// A call through a function value checks its arguments as a named call
// does (Part I 5.4 C, #1996): a function type's parameters are typed as a
// signature's, through a `let`, a parameter that only runs its code and a
// field that holds code, and the count too.
struct Act { f: fn(keep a: String) -> i64 }

fn eat(a: String) -> i64 { return a.len() }

fn via(f: fn(String) -> i64) -> i64 { return f(5) }

fn main() throws {
    let g: fn(String) -> i64 = eat
    println(g(5))
    println(via(eat))
    let a = Act { f: eat }
    println(a.f(5))
    println(g("a", "b"))
}
